1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,090
Lesson 8.6.

2
00:00:02,470 --> 00:00:05,850
Nonlinear Langevin
computers as classifiers.

3
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:10,731
Until now, thermodynamic
hardware computed at equilibrium:

4
00:00:10,731 --> 00:00:14,708
sampling a Boltzmann distribution
or solving a linear system.

5
00:00:15,088 --> 00:00:17,699
But equilibrating can
be slow, and not every

6
00:00:17,699 --> 00:00:20,726
problem can be written as
a Boltzmann distribution.

7
00:00:21,106 --> 00:00:24,642
Whitelam and Casert proposed
something else: a nonlinear

8
00:00:24,642 --> 00:00:28,116
Langevin computer trained to
give the right answer at a

9
00:00:28,116 --> 00:00:31,526
fixed instant, even if it
has not reached equilibrium.

10
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:37,021
The neuron is a single variable
fluctuating in contact with a heat bath,

11
00:00:37,401 --> 00:00:42,979
confined by the potential U(x, I) = J₂x² +

12
00:00:42,979 --> 00:00:48,292
J₃x³ + J₄x⁴ − I x, where I is the input.

13
00:00:48,672 --> 00:00:51,204
With only the quadratic term, the mean

14
00:00:51,204 --> 00:00:54,202
response is linear: at
equilibrium it is I/2.

15
00:00:54,582 --> 00:00:58,650
The quartic term makes it
nonlinear: the output saturates,

16
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:02,156
like a hyperbolic tangent,
near plus or minus one.

17
00:01:02,536 --> 00:01:06,512
Neurons are coupled by bilinear
terms, and form layers.

18
00:01:07,617 --> 00:01:09,844
The nonlinearity is not instantaneous.

19
00:01:10,224 --> 00:01:14,156
We simulate the quartic neuron
with β = 10 starting from zero.

20
00:01:14,536 --> 00:01:19,039
At time 0.1, the mean
response is still almost

21
00:01:19,039 --> 00:01:23,934
linear: 0.18 for an input
of two and 0.53 for six.

22
00:01:24,314 --> 00:01:31,416
At time one it already saturates: 0.57
and 0.99, almost the equilibrium values.

23
00:01:31,796 --> 00:01:35,010
The only requirement on the
readout time is that it be

24
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:37,808
long enough for the
activation to be nonlinear.

25
00:01:38,917 --> 00:01:44,256
Training fixes an observation time, t_f
= 1 in units of the relaxation time,

26
00:01:44,256 --> 00:01:49,033
and adjusts couplings and biases
so that the output at that instant,

27
00:01:49,413 --> 00:01:53,486
averaged over reset
trajectories, minimizes the loss.

28
00:01:53,866 --> 00:01:56,261
The authors use a genetic algorithm.

29
00:01:56,641 --> 00:02:01,047
The result is a computer programmed
to express the target function at

30
00:02:01,047 --> 00:02:05,389
the readout time, not at equilibrium:
in the cosine-fitting example,

31
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:09,029
the equilibrium output is very
different from the target.

32
00:02:09,409 --> 00:02:11,051
It operates far from equilibrium.

33
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:12,957
What was achieved?

34
00:02:13,337 --> 00:02:16,244
A network of only quadratic
neurons does not learn

35
00:02:16,244 --> 00:02:18,686
the cosine; with the quartic term it does.

36
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:22,504
On MNIST, a three-layer
network of thirty-two neurons

37
00:02:22,504 --> 00:02:26,137
classifies with about
ninety-three percent accuracy, not

38
00:02:26,137 --> 00:02:29,639
state of the art, but better
than a linear classifier,

39
00:02:30,019 --> 00:02:33,797
and about twenty samples per
input already reach that accuracy.

40
00:02:34,177 --> 00:02:37,654
Trained with an energy scale
ten times the thermal one, it

41
00:02:37,654 --> 00:02:40,591
works almost as well with
noise ten times larger.

42
00:02:41,717 --> 00:02:47,399
With a relaxation time of one microsecond,
as in circuit devices, a classification

43
00:02:47,399 --> 00:02:52,388
would take on the order of microseconds,
driven by thermal fluctuations.

44
00:02:52,768 --> 00:02:55,852
But we must be honest: everything
is simulation, training

45
00:02:55,852 --> 00:02:58,666
was much more expensive
than training a conventional

46
00:02:58,666 --> 00:03:01,589
network, and the paper reports
no energy measurements.

47
00:03:01,969 --> 00:03:06,918
The efficiency of inference protocols,
how much energy it costs to load the input

48
00:03:06,918 --> 00:03:11,318
and read the output, is an open
question we will study in project seven.

49
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:16,674
These ideas open the way to
physical neural networks with RLC

50
00:03:16,674 --> 00:03:21,245
circuits, Josephson junctions or
mechanical oscillators, where the

51
00:03:21,245 --> 00:03:25,123
readout time is a design
parameter and noise a resource.

52
00:03:25,503 --> 00:03:28,952
In project seven you will
train a computer of this kind.

53
00:03:30,083 --> 00:03:34,721
Common mistakes: assuming the trained
device computes at equilibrium.

54
00:03:35,101 --> 00:03:38,332
Reading the output before
the activation is nonlinear.

55
00:03:38,712 --> 00:03:41,279
Quoting an energy efficiency
that was not measured.

56
00:03:41,659 --> 00:03:43,605
And ignoring the cost of training.

57
00:03:44,717 --> 00:03:48,591
In summary: a thermodynamic
neuron with a quartic potential

58
00:03:48,591 --> 00:03:51,874
has a nonlinear activation
that appears over time.

59
00:03:52,254 --> 00:03:57,895
A network of them, trained for a fixed
readout time, classifies MNIST at about

60
00:03:57,895 --> 00:04:03,031
ninety-three percent operating out
of equilibrium, and tolerates noise.

61
00:04:03,411 --> 00:04:07,773
Hardware, energy measurements and cheaper
training methods are still missing.

62
00:04:08,883 --> 00:04:13,047
To practice: compute the equilibrium
mean of the quadratic neuron.

63
00:04:13,427 --> 00:04:16,577
Explain why the quartic
term saturates the response.

64
00:04:16,957 --> 00:04:19,943
Say what training for a
readout time optimizes.

65
00:04:20,323 --> 00:04:23,399
And name two missing pieces
before claiming efficiency.
